{
  "dom_id": "step-q102131",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Express $(-\\infty ,3]\\cup [6,\\infty )$ as an inequality.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we graph the two intervals on a number line:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191664/assets/ec4128763f85e601def705f1133bcff5cd7956cdcc10bfe438bd094a127c928a.png)\n\nThe interval $(-\\infty ,3]$ can be expressed as the inequality $x\\le 3.$\n\nLikewise, the interval $[6,\\infty )$ can be expressed as the inequality $x\\ge 6.$\n\nSo, the union of these intervals represents the compound inequality\n\n$$\nx\\le 3\\text{ or }x\\ge 6.\n$$",
